Mechanism. On AWS a storage credential is an IAM role whose trust policy lets a Databricks-side principal assume it, following the template on the current S3 subpage, with the role's permissions policy scoped to the bucket and prefixes. The documentation states the role must be defined in the same AWS account as the bucket. An external location pairs a path such as s3://cinderline-inspections/accepted/ with that credential; privileges on the credential control who may create locations, and privileges on the location (READ FILES, WRITE FILES) control direct path access. Tables registered under the location carry their own SELECT and MODIFY.

The two-step check for the nightly job, run as service principal job-nightly:

```sql
-- Synthetic teaching example; not executed.
-- SQL names a service principal by its application ID, not its display name:
-- the angle-bracket value stands for job-nightly's ID (a group holding it also works).
GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG quality TO `<job-nightly-application-id>`;
GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA quality.accepted TO `<job-nightly-application-id>`;
GRANT SELECT ON TABLE quality.accepted.inspections TO `<job-nightly-application-id>`;
SHOW GRANTS ON TABLE quality.accepted.inspections;
```

If any of the three is missing, the catalog answers PERMISSION_DENIED and names the missing privilege and the principal; no AWS call was made. If all three hold, Databricks assumes cinderline-lake-cred's role and calls S3. An AccessDenied here means the role's policy, its trust policy, or the bucket policy does not cover the prefix, and a KMS-worded denial means the key policy is missing the role.

What to inspect: the run-as principal of the job, SHOW GRANTS at all three levels, the external location's credential and path, the role ARN and account, the bucket policy statements. What this does not settle: whether the identity can reach the endpoint at all; a timeout is a different beat.
